WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
Two beakers, a hundred millilitres each.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.160
One is pure water, at pH seven.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.320
The other is a mix of ethanoic acid

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.520
and sodium ethanoate, at pH four point seven

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:12.560
four.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:15.120
Add one millilitre of one mole per litre

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.080
hydrochloric acid to each.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.680
The water drops to pH two point zero

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.760
zero, five whole units.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:24.960
The mixture drops to four point six six,

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:26.760
less than a tenth of a unit.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.440
So what's in that second beaker that soaks

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.520
up the acid, and can it ever run

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:32.080
out?

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.900
Ethanoic acid is weak, so most of it

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:37.620
stays as whole molecules.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:41.620
The sodium ethanoate dissolves completely, adding lots of

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.060
ethanoate ions, ethanoic acid's conjugate base.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:47.700
So the beaker holds a big supply of

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:49.340
both partners at once.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.420
A weak acid and its conjugate base, both

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.260
in large amounts: that's a buffer, a solution

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.580
that resists a change in pH when a

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:00.340
little acid or base is added.

00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:03.646
Now add the hydrochloric acid.

00:01:03.807 --> 00:01:06.926
It's strong, so it puts hydronium ions straight

00:01:07.007 --> 00:01:07.726
into the beaker.

00:01:08.207 --> 00:01:11.406
But here, each one meets an ethanoate ion,

00:01:11.447 --> 00:01:13.806
the conjugate base of a weak acid, so

00:01:13.887 --> 00:01:15.606
it pulls the proton back.

00:01:15.887 --> 00:01:19.926
Hydronium plus ethanoate gives ethanoic acid and water.

00:01:20.207 --> 00:01:23.326
Almost every added hydronium is used up, leaving

00:01:23.407 --> 00:01:25.646
a little more ethanoic acid and a little

00:01:25.727 --> 00:01:27.006
less ethanoate.

00:01:27.247 --> 00:01:30.046
In Module 5 terms, adding a product pushed

00:01:30.127 --> 00:01:32.046
the equilibrium back to the left.

00:01:33.267 --> 00:01:36.306
Add sodium hydroxide instead, and the other partner

00:01:36.387 --> 00:01:36.946
steps in.

00:01:37.427 --> 00:01:39.986
Each hydroxide ion takes a proton from an

00:01:40.067 --> 00:01:43.666
ethanoic acid molecule, making ethanoate and water.

00:01:44.067 --> 00:01:47.026
The same millilitre of sodium hydroxide takes water

00:01:47.107 --> 00:01:49.986
from seven to twelve, but the buffer only

00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:52.226
from four point seven four to four point

00:01:52.307 --> 00:01:53.426
eight three.

00:01:53.507 --> 00:01:55.906
So a buffer has a defender for each

00:01:55.987 --> 00:01:59.506
attack: the conjugate base takes on added acid,

00:01:59.547 --> 00:02:02.066
and the weak acid takes on added base.

00:02:03.267 --> 00:02:05.706
So why does the pH move at all?

00:02:05.987 --> 00:02:08.386
Go back to K a for ethanoic acid.

00:02:08.787 --> 00:02:12.226
Rearrange it, and hydronium equals K a times

00:02:12.387 --> 00:02:14.186
acid over ethanoate.

00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:17.186
Very little of either partner reacts with water,

00:02:17.307 --> 00:02:18.786
so you can use the amounts you put

00:02:18.867 --> 00:02:19.106
in.

00:02:19.587 --> 00:02:22.546
They share one volume, which cancels, so moles

00:02:22.667 --> 00:02:23.186
work too.

00:02:23.667 --> 00:02:26.466
Here there's zero point zero one zero zero

00:02:26.627 --> 00:02:29.346
moles of each, so the ratio is one,

00:02:29.427 --> 00:02:31.746
and hydronium just equals K a.

00:02:31.987 --> 00:02:33.826
So when the two partners are equal, the

00:02:33.907 --> 00:02:36.626
pH equals the p K a, four point

00:02:36.787 --> 00:02:37.506
seven four.

00:02:38.734 --> 00:02:41.293
Now add the zero point zero zero one

00:02:41.374 --> 00:02:43.213
zero moles of hydronium.

00:02:43.454 --> 00:02:46.813
It turns that much ethanoate into ethanoic acid,

00:02:46.894 --> 00:02:49.293
so the acid rises to zero point zero

00:02:49.534 --> 00:02:52.733
one one zero moles, and the ethanoate falls

00:02:52.814 --> 00:02:55.613
to zero point zero zero nine zero.

00:02:55.654 --> 00:02:58.413
The ratio becomes about one point two, so

00:02:58.494 --> 00:03:01.773
hydronium rises by only about twenty per cent,

00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:04.653
and the pH is four point six six.

00:03:04.734 --> 00:03:07.773
In plain water, nothing takes up the hydronium,

00:03:07.854 --> 00:03:10.653
so it rises a hundred thousand times.

00:03:11.867 --> 00:03:14.586
That ratio sets the pH of any buffer.

00:03:14.867 --> 00:03:17.626
More acid than base puts the pH below

00:03:17.707 --> 00:03:20.026
the p K a, more base puts it

00:03:20.267 --> 00:03:20.746
above.

00:03:20.987 --> 00:03:23.386
And a buffer doesn't have to be neutral:

00:03:23.467 --> 00:03:25.786
this one sits at four point seven four.

00:03:26.027 --> 00:03:28.986
But hydrochloric acid with sodium chloride is not

00:03:29.067 --> 00:03:29.626
a buffer.

00:03:30.107 --> 00:03:32.426
Chloride is the conjugate base of a strong

00:03:32.507 --> 00:03:35.146
acid, so it doesn't take protons back.

00:03:36.367 --> 00:03:39.006
Now keep adding acid, a millilitre at a

00:03:39.087 --> 00:03:39.766
time.

00:03:40.047 --> 00:03:42.926
Each portion uses up zero point zero zero

00:03:43.007 --> 00:03:45.406
one zero moles of ethanoate.

00:03:45.727 --> 00:03:48.326
At first, the pH falls slowly.

00:03:48.607 --> 00:03:51.286
But by about nine millilitres, almost all the

00:03:51.327 --> 00:03:54.126
ethanoate is gone, and at ten, there's none

00:03:54.207 --> 00:03:54.926
left.

00:03:55.247 --> 00:03:57.566
Nothing can take the next hydronium, so the

00:03:57.647 --> 00:03:59.286
pH plunges.

00:03:59.727 --> 00:04:01.646
How much acid or base a buffer can

00:04:01.727 --> 00:04:04.366
take before one partner is nearly used up

00:04:04.407 --> 00:04:05.966
is its capacity.

00:04:06.007 --> 00:04:07.886
It depends on how much of each partner

00:04:07.967 --> 00:04:10.046
there is, not on the pH.

00:04:11.267 --> 00:04:13.746
Your blood stays between pH seven point three

00:04:13.907 --> 00:04:17.266
five and seven point four five, because enzymes

00:04:17.307 --> 00:04:20.466
and haemoglobin work best in a narrow range.

00:04:20.787 --> 00:04:24.306
Its main buffer pairs carbonic acid with hydrogencarbonate

00:04:24.467 --> 00:04:25.226
ions.

00:04:25.507 --> 00:04:27.786
When muscles working hard add acid to the

00:04:27.827 --> 00:04:31.986
blood, hydrogencarbonate takes up the hydronium, making carbonic

00:04:32.067 --> 00:04:32.466
acid.

00:04:33.027 --> 00:04:35.906
That breaks down into carbon dioxide and water,

00:04:35.947 --> 00:04:38.546
and your lungs breathe the carbon dioxide out.

00:04:39.767 --> 00:04:43.126
Blood has about twenty hydrogencarbonate ions for each

00:04:43.167 --> 00:04:45.366
dissolved carbon dioxide molecule.

00:04:45.687 --> 00:04:48.646
Carbon dioxide and carbonic acid together are the

00:04:48.807 --> 00:04:50.286
acid side of this buffer.

00:04:50.567 --> 00:04:52.406
So it can take up far more acid

00:04:52.487 --> 00:04:55.126
than base, which suits a body whose metabolism

00:04:55.207 --> 00:04:56.646
mostly makes acid.

00:04:56.887 --> 00:04:59.886
And unlike a beaker, it's topped up: the

00:04:59.927 --> 00:05:02.486
lungs adjust how much carbon dioxide they breathe

00:05:02.567 --> 00:05:06.166
out within minutes, and the kidneys adjust hydrogencarbonate

00:05:06.327 --> 00:05:07.686
over hours to days.

00:05:08.327 --> 00:05:11.366
Inside cells, a phosphate buffer helps too, and

00:05:11.447 --> 00:05:13.046
in the oceans, carbonate.

00:05:14.267 --> 00:05:15.626
So here's what to remember.

00:05:16.107 --> 00:05:18.986
A buffer has a defender for each attack:

00:05:19.067 --> 00:05:21.866
the conjugate base takes added acid, the weak

00:05:21.947 --> 00:05:23.786
acid takes added base.

00:05:24.027 --> 00:05:26.986
And a buffer resists, it doesn't prevent.

00:05:27.227 --> 00:05:29.306
Add enough to use up one partner, and

00:05:29.387 --> 00:05:30.266
it fails.

00:05:31.467 --> 00:05:32.186
Your turn.

00:05:32.667 --> 00:05:35.786
A solution contains zero point two zero moles

00:05:35.867 --> 00:05:39.066
per litre ammonia and zero point two zero

00:05:39.147 --> 00:05:41.626
moles per litre ammonium chloride.

00:05:41.947 --> 00:05:45.466
Explain, using equations, how it resists a change

00:05:45.547 --> 00:05:48.426
in pH when a little hydrochloric acid or

00:05:48.587 --> 00:05:50.426
sodium hydroxide is added.

00:05:51.067 --> 00:05:53.946
Then estimate its pH, given that the p

00:05:54.067 --> 00:05:56.186
K a of ammonium is nine point two

00:05:56.347 --> 00:05:57.306
five.

00:05:57.467 --> 00:05:58.666
Pause here to answer.

00:06:09.067 --> 00:06:11.706
Ammonia is the base partner, so it takes

00:06:11.787 --> 00:06:16.346
the added hydronium: ammonia plus hydronium gives ammonium

00:06:16.467 --> 00:06:17.546
and water.

00:06:17.627 --> 00:06:20.106
Ammonium is the acid partner, so it gives

00:06:20.187 --> 00:06:23.466
a proton to added hydroxide, making ammonia and

00:06:23.547 --> 00:06:24.426
water.

00:06:24.507 --> 00:06:26.506
The two are equal, so the pH is

00:06:26.667 --> 00:06:28.906
about the p K a, nine point two

00:06:29.107 --> 00:06:29.866
five.

00:06:30.067 --> 00:06:32.026
Writing ammonium as the one that takes up

00:06:32.187 --> 00:06:34.346
added acid swaps the partners.

00:06:35.567 --> 00:06:36.206
Last one.

00:06:36.767 --> 00:06:39.086
Buffers X and Y each have a volume

00:06:39.167 --> 00:06:41.726
of a hundred millilitres and a pH of

00:06:41.807 --> 00:06:43.646
four point seven four.

00:06:43.727 --> 00:06:46.926
X contains zero point zero one zero moles

00:06:47.007 --> 00:06:50.606
each of ethanoic acid and sodium ethanoate.

00:06:50.687 --> 00:06:53.886
Y contains zero point zero zero one zero

00:06:53.967 --> 00:06:54.766
moles of each.

00:06:55.407 --> 00:06:58.286
A student adds zero point zero zero two

00:06:58.367 --> 00:07:01.166
zero moles of hydrochloric acid to each.

00:07:01.487 --> 00:07:04.126
Predict and explain the pH change in each

00:07:04.207 --> 00:07:05.326
solution.

00:07:05.487 --> 00:07:06.766
Pause here to answer.

00:07:17.167 --> 00:07:19.646
X takes up all the added acid, with

00:07:19.767 --> 00:07:22.286
zero point zero zero eight zero moles of

00:07:22.407 --> 00:07:24.126
ethanoate still left.

00:07:24.167 --> 00:07:26.766
The ratio becomes one point five, so the

00:07:26.847 --> 00:07:29.966
pH falls only to four point five seven.

00:07:30.047 --> 00:07:32.446
In Y, half the acid uses up all

00:07:32.527 --> 00:07:35.726
its ethanoate, and the rest stays as hydronium:

00:07:35.807 --> 00:07:38.046
the pH drops to about two.

00:07:38.247 --> 00:07:41.246
Thinking equal pH means equal protection is the

00:07:41.327 --> 00:07:45.086
slip: pH shows the ratio, not the amounts.

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:49.180
For any buffer: find the weak acid and

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:50.660
its conjugate base.

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:53.820
Added acid reacts with the base partner, added

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:55.780
base with the acid partner.

00:07:56.060 --> 00:07:57.940
Get the pH from K a and the

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:00.900
ratio, and check neither has run out.

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:03.980
Next: preparing a buffer in the lab, and

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:05.660
testing it against water.

00:08:06.867 --> 00:08:08.306
Here are the key notes.

00:08:08.547 --> 00:08:10.466
Pause the video if you need more time

00:08:10.547 --> 00:08:11.346
to copy them.
